From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 02:29:08 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] drm/i915: the empty HDMI vendor InfoFrame as a choice
 (hdmi_empty_vsif)

DRM fills the HDMI vendor InfoFrame for every mode of a sink whose EDID
has the HDMI vendor block, also where it carries neither an HDMI VIC
nor 3D (drm_hdmi_vendor_infoframe_from_display_mode(); Linux
f1781e9bb2dd: HDMI 2.0 Appendix F suggests that the source keeps
sending it as it leaves 3D, for sinks that do not leave 3D when it just
stops coming, and HDMI 1.4 makes it optional there), and i915 sends it.

Issue #28 (corgeone): an Anthem AVM 90 between an i7-1355U and an LG G2
takes the frame rate from that InfoFrame whenever there is one, and an
empty one does not update it. At 2160p60 (VIC 97, vblanks measured at
59.998 Hz) it says 4K30, the last mode that had an HDMI VIC, in its own
information and over its IP control; the same mode with the Dolby VSIF
in that slot (Dolby Vision for the menus), or an Apple TV, which sends
none, is shown as 4K60.

The parameter i915.hdmi_empty_vsif (xe.hdmi_empty_vsif) is on by
default, as upstream. Off, the HDMI vendor InfoFrame goes out only where
it carries something, as AMD's display core sends it
(mod_build_hf_vsif_infopacket()), and:
- in the mode that follows a 3D one it goes out empty, for as long as
  that mode lasts (Appendix F, as Hades' amdgpu does since linux-9919);
- within a mode the vendor DIP stays as it is on the screen: a change of
  infoframes.enable needs a modeset, which a commit that keeps the mode
  (HDR metadata, a colour property, HDR10+) must not be made to need. So
  it is decided when the mode changes, and a change of the parameter at
  run time applies from the next mode;
- HDR10+ takes the DIP as before (linux-9954). Where no empty InfoFrame
  is under it, its start and its end within a mode turn the DIP on and
  off in a fastset: the fastset check lets infoframes.enable differ in
  the vendor bit for exactly that (intel_hdmi_vsif_fastset_vendor()),
  and intel_hdmi_fastset_hdr10plus() turns the DIP off where HDR10+
  ended, as hsw_write_infoframe() turns it on where it starts.
The Dolby VSIF of the tunnel and HDR over the tunnel do not change.
With the parameter on nothing changes: no state drops the InfoFrame, so
neither the decision nor the fastset rule ever applies.

The kernel log says it at each connector when the parameter is off, and
once for each new mode where it acts ("not sent", or "sent empty ... as
it follows a 3D one"); linux-9979 says the vendor InfoFrame going off.

KUnit (drm_hdmi_vsif): the decision in every case; a run of modes as
issue #28 and a 3D film make them (2160p30, the menus at 2160p60, a
commit that keeps that mode, 1080p24 3D, the menus at 1080p60 and a
commit that keeps it, 2160p60), with the parameter on and off; and the
fastsets that may turn the DIP on and off.
